WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.601
(upbeat drum and flute music)

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.462
- [Narrator] For centuries,
Koreans have believed

00:00:03.462 --> 00:00:04.890
in the medicinal properties

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.104
of a folk remedy called jukyeom,

00:00:07.104 --> 00:00:09.354
or in English, bamboo salt.

00:00:19.997 --> 00:00:23.103
Bamboo salt is ubiquitous in South Korea.

00:00:23.103 --> 00:00:25.294
It is made by repeatedly roasting salt

00:00:25.294 --> 00:00:27.189
in stalks of bamboo.

00:00:27.189 --> 00:00:30.038
Its adherents claim it combats disease,

00:00:30.038 --> 00:00:31.645
indigestion and pain.

00:00:31.645 --> 00:00:35.312
But what's certain is
it's not easy to make.

00:00:45.856 --> 00:00:47.410
The definitive method was fashioned

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.129
in the early 20th century

00:00:49.129 --> 00:00:52.770
by a famed medicine man named Kim Ilhoon.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.031
Near the end of Kim Ilhoon's life,

00:00:55.031 --> 00:00:58.601
his son, Kim Yoonse, established
a bamboo salt company

00:00:58.601 --> 00:01:00.570
that preserved and industrialized

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.237
the family's salt-making method.

00:01:55.527 --> 00:01:59.330
For Kim, the three-year
process is worth every minute.

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:00.686
Producing it is a way to honor

00:02:00.686 --> 00:02:03.059
both this uniquely Korean remedy,

00:02:03.059 --> 00:02:05.121
and his father's legacy.

00:02:05.121 --> 00:02:09.169
(airy, cheerful music)

00:02:09.169 --> 00:02:12.086
(electronic tone)

